    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gunston Hall’s mission is to stimulate exploration of principles George Mason expressed in the 1776 Virginia Declaration of Rights, covering 550 acres today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.greatamericantreasures.org/destinations/gunston-hall/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Gunston Hall is the historic Georgian‑style home of George Mason IV, author of the Fairfax Resolves and the Virginia Declaration of Rights.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.greatamericantreasures.org/destinations/gunston-hall/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1787, George Mason was named one of Virginia’s delegates to the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia, his only long trip outside the state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Mason",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mason’s Virginia Declaration of Rights, drafted in 1776, served as the foundation for the United States Bill of Rights.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Mason",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mason’s writings, including the Fairfax Resolves and his Objections to the Constitution, profoundly shaped American political thought.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
